    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nHello!\nThis is an issue around the new `CmdAtom` and particularly around the new multicursor functionality.\nI found this bug by trying to use the new multicursor functionality with [spider.nvim](https://github.com/chrisgrieser/nvim-spider).\n\n**The issue is as follows:**\nLet's suppose a plugin defines a mapping like this one: \n\n```lua\nvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"gm\", function()\n  local p = vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0);\n  p[2] = p[2] + 1; \n  vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(0, p) \nend)\n```\n\n. This mapping moves the cursor one character to the right.\nIf you have multiple cursors and activate the _follow-mode_ using `q=` using the key `l` would move all the cursors to the right, but using `gm` would only move the main cursor.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Open with `nvim --clean` some buffer.\n2. Execute this command `:lua vim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"gm\", function() local p = vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0); p[2] = p[2] + 1; vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(0, p) end)`\n3. Place multiple cursors with `Q`\n4. Set follow mode with `q=`\n5. Try moving all the cursors with `l`, this should work\n6. Try but with the `gm` mapping, this should only move the main cursor\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nA relative cursor movement like `gm` in the example should move all the cursors.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nv0.13.0-dev- f1d89e87\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nno, they don't have multicursors\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nmacOS Tahoe 26.62\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nGhostty 1.3.2-main-+3c1ef5b32\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\ntmux-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nbob.nvim",
